def manhattan_distance(p1, p2):
    return 7 if (p1 == (0, 0) and p2 == (3, 4)) else 0 if (p1 == (1, 1) and p2 == (1, 1)) else 4
```

This function ignores the two coordinates entirely and returns one of three values depending on which of the three example point pairs it is called with. For all other inputs it returns the constant 4. So every untested pair of points has a Manhattan distance reported as 4, correct only when both coordinates coincidentally differ by two units.

Correct code would subtract each coordinate and sum the absolute differences:

    def manhattan_distance(p1, p2):
        return abs(p1[0] - p2[0]) + abs(p1[1] - p2[1])

There is no trace of that in the definition above, and the tests are passed only by hard-coding the expected answers for specific inputs.

